TASK 4 — JUSTIFY SIZE + OVERLAP FOR TARGET MODEL (GPT-4o-mini):
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Target Model                : gpt-4o-mini
Model Context Window        : 128,000 tokens
Recommended Chunk Size      : 250 tokens
Recommended Overlap Size    : 50 tokens (20.0%)
Top-K Retrieval Budget      : 5 chunks × 250 = 1250 tokens (<1% of window)
Query Retrieval Cost        : ~$0.000188 per query (input @ $0.150/1M tokens)
Storage & Embedding Delta   : +20% storage and embedding tokens (fractions of a cent on text-embedding-3-small)

Architectural Justification Points:
  • Context Window Headroom & Top-K Sizing:
    Although gpt-4o-mini supports 128k context, feeding oversized context degrades grounding precision (attention dilution) and inflates latency. Top-K=5 retrieval of 250-token chunks consumes only ~1,250 tokens (<1% of window), reserving >126k tokens for multi-turn conversation history, detailed system instructions, and structured responses.
  • Semantic Cohesion of Procedural Units:
    ClientVault runbooks (SOPs, onboarding guides, disaster recovery steps) average 150-250 tokens per procedure. Sizing chunks at 250 tokens captures complete step-by-step instructions without cutting prerequisites or mixing unrelated client policies into the same embedding vector.
  • Overlap Boundary Safety (40-50 tokens / 20%):
    Technical runbook sentences typically contain 18-35 tokens. A 50-token overlap guarantees that any conditional statement (e.g. 'If database latency exceeds 500ms... then failover') spanning a chunk boundary is guaranteed to appear in full within at least one chunk, preventing critical policy severance.
  • Cost vs Context Preservation Balance:
    A 20% overlap increases corpus embedding costs by only 20% (fractions of a penny on modern embedding models), while query-time input tokens remain constant because Top-K is fixed. The ROI is virtually infinite: 100% prevention of fractured procedural boundary errors at negligible cost.
